AB  - A diverse range of opioids and enantiomers were examined for their ability to displace binding at the [H-3] MK-801-labelled site of the N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor in rat forebrain, displacement which is equatable with noncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist activity. Surprisingly, d-morphine, but not natural I-morphine, has low micromolar affinity for the site, suggesting clinical potential for racemic dl-morphine in the treatment of neuropathic pain with reduced development of tolerance. The opioid mu -receptor agonists: levorphanol, d- and dl-methadone, displayed similar properties. With the exception of the case of d-morphine, the structural requirements for affinity correspond closely with those previously found for the inhibitory effects of opioids on monoamine uptake. (C) 2000 Elsevier Science Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.
