AB  - Noxious stimulation always results in changes in gene expression within the central nervous system, and different chronic pain states generate unique neurochemical signatures in the brain and spinal cord. Functional imaging and stimulation studies have shown that multiple cortical areas are activated by painful stimuli. In the present study, we investigated the changes in glial function associated with emotionality under a neruropathic pain-like state. By the microinjection of a retrograde tracer, fluoro-gold (FG) into the cingulate cortex (CG) of rats, numerous FG-labeled cells were apparently detected in the thalamus. Furthermore, c-fos-like immunoreactivity was increased by sciatic nerve ligation and overlapped with the FG-positive neuron in the thalamus. Here, we found that chronic pain decreases the GABA level at the synaptic cleft associated with an increase in GABA uptake mainly through GAT-3 (mouse GAT4) located on activated astrocytes and diminishes the efficiency of GABAergic neurotransmission in the CG of mice associated with the expression of emotional dysfunction. We also found that the microinjection of either astrocyte, activated astrocyte or astrocyte-conditioned medium into the CG of mice aggravated the expression of emotional dysfunction. Moreover, chronic pain induced astrocyte activation in the CG with no change in the neural or glial differentiation, and the migration of neural stem cells from the subventricular zone. These findings suggest that consecutive noxious stimuli transmitted from the spinal cord to the CG via the thalamus of rodents under a neuropathic pain-like state could lead to the activation of astrocytes with an increase in GAT associated with diminished efficiency of GABAergic neurotransmission in the CG, which in turn expresses the emotional dysfunction in rodents with chronic noxious stimuli.
