AB  - Introduction: Interferon-beta (IFN-beta) has been used for the treatment of multiple sclerosis, because of its immunomodulatory activity. IFN-beta can also improve nerve regeneration after crush injuries. We aimed to investigate the potential of using IFN-beta to treat peripheral nerve injury and the role of pro-inflammatory mediators and apoptosis in the effect of IFN-beta.
   Materials and methods: Fifty male rats, weighing 170-200 g, were randomized into five groups of 10 each: control (no injury), axonotmesis, axonotmesis+IFN-beta, neurotmesis, and neurotmesis+IFN-beta. The experimental trauma was performed on the sciatic nerve in anesthetized rats (intraperitoneal thiopental 25 mg/kg). IFN-beta was applied intraperitoneally for 28 days (4x 103 IU/kg per day). At 4 weeks after the surgical procedure, the right sciatic nerves were dissected out for histopathological, quantitative (density of myelinated axons), and molecular (mRNA expression of IL-1 beta and caspase-3) analysis.
   Results: Many axons with severe damage and big gaps between axons were observed in sciatic nerve sections in the axonottnesis group. In the neurotmesis group, the axons were irregular in appearance and axon degeneration, from mild to severe, was observed. In both groups, the IFN-beta treatment improved nerve healing significantly. IFN-beta also decreased caspase 3 levels and IL-1 beta in traumatic nerves, suggesting anti-apoptotic and anti-inflammatory effects of IFN-beta.
   Conclusion: IFN-beta may be a new therapeutic approach for the treatment of peripheral nerve injury; Further experimental studies are required to clarify the mechanisms of action of IFN-beta, and clinical studies are required to determine optimum treatment doses and treatment durations for nerve injuries.
